        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "9e53cfaa-f8b6-376b-ac11-06acab7f32ba",
                "word": "Lysistrata",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d1b79502-a9fe-337b-bb29-652891995885",
                        "entry_id": "9e53cfaa-f8b6-376b-ac11-06acab7f32ba",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A comedy by Ancient Greek playwright Aristophanes, a comic account of one woman's (Lysistrata) extraordinary mission to end the Peloponnesian War by denying men sex."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "94e22dc1-2577-4ee7-990b-9ccc40d54afc",
                                "sense_id": "d1b79502-a9fe-337b-bb29-652891995885",
                                "text": "The authors have drawn to some extent on the Lysistrata to aid them, but have fallen as far short of the fun as of the indecency of that memorable play."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7471180a-371d-406e-a13e-baa158fdc19c",
                                "sense_id": "d1b79502-a9fe-337b-bb29-652891995885",
                                "text": "The idea of a sex strike is as old as Aristophanes. Undoubtedly after a tiff with Mrs. Aristophanes, he wrote his “Lysistrata,” a comedy about wives who end the Peloponnesian War by resisting their husbands."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1e52ea4d-67d0-4e29-b9e3-70646a67a49c",
                                "sense_id": "d1b79502-a9fe-337b-bb29-652891995885",
                                "text": "So I created the same women and started with the same story, but then just played it out in a contemporary setting. Also, unlike in “Lysistrata,” the sex strike doesn’t work."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "25b75a1f-36ac-4a1a-a2f9-ad75130a9f5b",
                                "sense_id": "d1b79502-a9fe-337b-bb29-652891995885",
                                "text": "Making sex a quid for some quo can be, as in “Lysistrata,” a dramatic strategy, but it probably isn’t the best one."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0ff9a0e4-b61b-46ee-9f99-b87896963b10",
                                "sense_id": "d1b79502-a9fe-337b-bb29-652891995885",
                                "text": "Seeing our sexual culture through this data, one might think we are at war, and Lysistrata a real person."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3dfa8561-fffd-31bf-a711-6c23c249befa",
                        "entry_id": "9e53cfaa-f8b6-376b-ac11-06acab7f32ba",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A female given name from Ancient Greek."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncommon"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff689866-7603-4555-9cf4-d5134ca2d6a5",
                                "sense_id": "3dfa8561-fffd-31bf-a711-6c23c249befa",
                                "text": "I drop the note on the floor and take another sheet and write \"YES. Lysistrata Lee.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
